INT. LAST ELEVATOR, SHUTTERED DEPARTMENT STORE - NIGHT

Brass doors. A flickering floor indicator. A velvet-lined car smelling of mothballs. Three people stand elbow to elbow.

MARCELLUS "MARCEL" DUPRÉ-IKEDA (50s, trench coat, briefcase) stares at the doors. HONORA FITZGERALD-ADEYEMI (30s, security uniform, flashlight) holds the stop button. And JASPER KOWALSKI-RHEE (20s, a stolen mannequin arm tucked under his jacket) sweats.

MARCEL
You said you'd have the floor cleared.

HONORA
I said I'd clear the floor. I didn't say anything about the elevator.

JASPER
Can someone please explain why this is moving?

HONORA
It's not supposed to.

MARCEL
It's been disconnected since Easter.

JASPER
Then who called it?

HONORA
Nobody called it.

MARCEL
Somebody pressed the button.

JASPER
Not me.

HONORA
Not me.

MARCEL
Not me.

The indicator flickers: 3... 2... 1... B.

JASPER
We don't have a basement.

HONORA
We do have a basement.

MARCEL
We have a sub-basement.

JASPER
What's in it?

MARCEL
The vault.

HONORA
The old vault. They sealed it in 1986.

JASPER
What's in the vault?

MARCEL
Nothing. Everyone knows that. There's nothing in the vault.

The doors slide open. A cold exhale of air. Beyond them, lit by a single bulb, sits a velvet armchair, and in it a mannequin in a doorman's coat, one hand missing.

JASPER
(whispering)
That's the one I took.

HONORA
The arm or the mannequin?

JASPER
The arm. I took the arm.

MARCEL
Put it back.

JASPER
Back where?

MARCEL
On him. Go on.

Jasper steps out. The doors begin to close behind him.
